 "steps": [
  {
   "id": "s0",
   "title": "Paris, 1824",
   "text": "Sadi Carnot, a 28-year-old French army engineer (born 1796), publishes one slim book, “Reflections on the Motive Power of Fire”. Steam engines were changing the world, but nobody knew **how much work** a given amount of heat could possibly give, or what the best engine would look like.",
   "actions": [],
   "dwell": 7000
  },
  {
   "id": "s1",
   "title": "The waterwheel picture",
   "text": "Water falling from a high pool to a low pool turns a wheel. The work depends on **how far** the water drops, not on what the wheel is made of. Move the sliders: a bigger drop \\(T_1-T_2\\) means more work per unit of water.",
   "actions": [],
   "dwell": 7000
  },
  {
   "id": "s2",
   "title": "Heat falls too",
   "text": "Carnot saw that an engine needs **two** temperatures: it works because heat falls from a hot body to a cold one, like water between pools. And the best possible engine must be **reversible**. From this came his theorem: the best efficiency depends only on \\(T_1\\) and \\(T_2\\), not on steam, air or anything else.",
   "actions": [],
   "dwell": 7500
  },
  {
   "id": "s3",
   "title": "What he got wrong",
   "text": "Carnot believed heat (\"caloric\") was like water: never used up, so the same amount leaves at the bottom as enters at the top. But some heat **becomes work**. By the 1840s Joule and others showed energy is conserved: \\(Q_1-Q_2=W\\). With \\(T_1={{T1|0}}\\) K and \\(T_2={{T2|0}}\\) K, \\(1000\\) J in leaves \\(Q_2={{Q2|0}}\\) J out and gives \\(W={{W|0}}\\) J.",
   "actions": [],
   "dwell": 8000
  },
  {
   "id": "s4",
   "title": "What came next",
   "text": "The book was almost ignored. Carnot died of cholera in 1832, aged 36. In 1834 Émile Clapeyron drew Carnot's cycle as a **P–V loop**, the graph you have been studying. Kelvin and Clausius then fixed the caloric mistake and built the **second law of thermodynamics** on Carnot's idea (1850s).",
   "actions": [],
   "dwell": 8000
  }
 ],
